Fillets: a rounding whose chain ends past a concave corner of its wall,
where its contact on the wall has gone on round the corner and leaves it
past the spine's end vertex, after its contact on the top has left the top
across a crease into the face at the end: the blend runs on over both
faces' surfaces and the faces at the end cut it (mitcad#1425).

The case: a bar's front wall turns through a concave corner rounded with 3
(a cylinder about the vertical) into the side of a tip whose top edge is
rounded with 4, a rounding that goes on round the corner up to the wall
as a corner patch tangent to the wall. The bar's top slopes by 2.2
degrees to the tip's and meets it along a crease. The wall's top edge and
the corner patch's edge with the bar's top are rounded with 5, the chain
ending at the crease. The ball's contact on the wall goes on round the
corner's cylinder below the corner patch; its contact on the top crosses
the crease 0.7 before the vertex at the end; its contact on the cylinder
reaches the cylinder's edge with the tip's side (tangent to it) 3 past
the vertex. The walk then went on along the tip's side, the other contact
standing on the crease, and the corner at the vertex failed: the walk
round the vertex followed the cylinder's boundary, which does not reach
the vertex ("the max number of faces reached"). The stored body runs the
ball on over the cylinder's and the top's surfaces and cuts the blend
with the faces it runs into: the tip's top beyond the crease, the corner
patch (leaving a sliver of it), the tip's rounding of 4 and the tip's
side.

- ComputeData: where, on the walk on past the spine's end, a contact
  reaches an edge of its face into a face that goes on from it
  (SearchFace), neither face holding the spine's end vertex (the contact
  has run off the spine's faces), more than 10 tolesp past the spine's
  end, after the other contact left its face into a face at the end (the
  walk ran it on unclassified), that contact ends on its edge too and the
  walk goes on with both sides unclassified: the end is the blend's, for
  the corner to cut. Where the contact's face holds the vertex, it meets
  the face at the end there and 1430's roll on the other contact's edge
  (ChFi3d_WalkedOnPastEndEdge) builds the end, as before.
- ChFi3d_CutRunOnExtension (new; ChFi3d_CutSingularExtension leaves such
  an end): the line is kept past the later contact's edge while its
  contacts run at most eight times as fast along the guide as at the
  spine's end, measured along the guide's length. The guide's extension
  past the first end was not parametrised by length (its derivative grew
  from 1 to 4), and the walk round the corner runs into the singular
  section where the guide's normal plane comes tangent to the ball centre's
  curve: twice as fast per unit of the guide's parameter cut the bottom
  chain's line short of its contact's edge.
- PerformIntersectionAtEnd: where F1, the face of side 1's contact, does
  not hold the end vertex, the walk along F1's boundary from that
  contact's edge stops at the first face that holds the vertex (the corner
  patch, after the tip's rounding of 4, found between the faces at the
  corner's top vertex as before), and the faces at the end go on round
  the vertex from there up to the face beyond side 2's edge
  (RoundVertexToEdge): the tip's side, its rounding of 4, the corner patch
  and the tip's top.

On the design's stored body before the item, with 1581, both chains: valid
and closed, the stored body's 127 faces; about each end the result on the
stored body and the stored body on it within 0.0007 mm, along each chain
within 0.0036 mm (the torus's vanishing ends, fitted in the file); the
change of volume -200.2687 against the file's -200.2866 mm3. Before, the
corners at both ends failed.

